Orvane Connection Licence — version 1.0, 18 November 2026 (English translation, not legally binding;
only the French text above is)
Copyright © StoneValley. All rights reserved, except what this licence allows.

Orvane is published so that people can read it and connect a game to it, not to be reused. In return for
the world it receives, each connected game sends back what its players do in it.

1. Terms. "Orvane": the software in this repository and everything it publishes (site, worlds,
snapshots, pieces, plates, herbarium). "The World": a world Orvane hosts and publishes, first of all the
world of Orvane at https://stonevalleyoff-ai.github.io/orvane/. "A Connected Game": a game, app or
interactive service registered on Orvane (it has a game key), that draws its creatures from the World
and complies with section 4. "The Connection Pieces": schemas/, types/, tampon/, kits/,
traducteurs/exemple/, index.js, outils/verifier.js, outils/banc-traducteur.js, outils/types.js,
monde/silhouette.js, monde/herbier.js, and the documentation on connecting a game. "World Content":
what the World publishes (lineages, names, values, silhouettes, plates, herbarium, chronicle, museum).
"Pressures": the batches described in schemas/pression.schema.json, docs/4 and docs/50.

2. Principle. Orvane belongs to StoneValley. Anything this licence does not expressly allow requires
StoneValley's written consent.

3. What is allowed, free of charge, worldwide, non-exclusively, until the licence ends (section 7).
3.1 Anyone may browse the site, the observatory and the documentation, and read the code to understand
Orvane and check what it does.
3.2 The publisher of a Connected Game, for that game only, may: (a) read the World and turn it into the
game's creatures, including in a commercial or closed-source game; (b) use World Content in the game to
show players the lineages and what happens to them; (c) copy the Connection Pieces into the game, adapt
them and ship them with it, as source or compiled; (d) use the orvane package (index.js and what it
loads, engine included) in the game, including to run a copy of the World (the solo world,
moteur/solo.js), provided the engine is not modified, not extracted from the game and not offered
separately; (e) develop and test the game by depositing its batches in the test world ("essai").

4. In return: send the Pressures back. The World is shared: every game that uses it gives back what its
players do in it. A Connected Game: (a) deposits, with its own game key and into the World it reads, its
players' Pressures: encounters with creatures drawn from the World, and every contract action the game
allows on them (kill, capture, release, feeding, moving, and the others), per
schemas/pression.schema.json and docs/4; (b) deposits them regularly: at least once for every World
cycle in which players played; offline play is deposited when the game is back online; (c) deposits true
counts: nothing invented, inflated, deliberately left out, deposited on behalf of another game, and none
of the game's own creatures (characters, monsters, bosses that do not come from the World); (d) does not
circumvent the buffer or its caps, for instance with several keys for one game or made-up accounts.
Batches deposited in "essai" no longer meet this obligation once the game is open to players (release,
early access, open beta). A game where players do not act on the creatures, and which only shows the
World, needs StoneValley's written consent.

5. What is not allowed, without StoneValley's written consent: (a) copying, reusing, modifying,
translating, adapting, redistributing, selling, renting or sublicensing Orvane or any part of it outside
section 3, in particular the engine, the genesis, the fusion, the hosting, the observatory, the benches
and the worlds; (b) running the engine outside a Connected Game, or running any world in it other than a
copy of the World; (c) using Orvane, its code or World Content to create or provide a competing product:
another ecosystem or evolution engine, another shared world for games to connect to, a renamed copy of
Orvane; (d) removing or altering StoneValley's and this licence's notices.

6. Notices. Each Connection Piece copied into a game keeps, or is given, at its top or next to it:
"Copyright StoneValley — Orvane Connection Licence
(https://github.com/stonevalleyoff-ai/orvane/blob/main/LICENSE)".

7. Termination. If a Connected Game does not comply, in particular with section 4, StoneValley may
notify it, at the contact given at registration or publicly if none was given. If it does not comply
within 30 days, the licence ends for that game: it stops reading the World and distributing the
Connection Pieces and the package, and StoneValley may suspend its key. A deliberate breach of section 5
ends the licence at once.

8. Earlier versions. What was published before 18 November 2026 was published under PolyForm Shield
1.0.0; copies obtained before that date remain under it. Everything published since is under this
licence. StoneValley may publish a new version of this licence; it applies to what is published after it.

9. Statutory rights. Nothing here removes what the law grants a lawful user of software (in France,
article L122-6-1 of the Intellectual Property Code).

10. No warranty. Orvane and the World are provided as is, without warranty, to the extent the law allows.
StoneValley is not liable for damage arising from their use, within the limits the law allows. The World
may change, pause or stop.

11. Governing law. French law. The French text is the binding one.

12. Other uses. For any use this licence does not allow, ask StoneValley:
https://github.com/stonevalleyoff-ai/orvane/issues

Third-party elements keep their own licence: the fonts in jeux/lisiere/polices/ are under the SIL Open
Font License 1.1.
